    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "A thesis on Oscar Wilde's \"Salome\" (1891) that critically examines and reworks the history of criticism around the play. Against traditional feminist interpretations, it offers a new reading that rejects the view of Salome as a simply powerful or successful individual. Rather, as Salome moves toward greater feminist agency, she must also engage fundamentally with her aesthetic and social constraints. Combining the feminist and aesthetic lens, then, produces a much-needed nuance to Salome's character analysis as both a woman and transgressor.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Acosta, Andrea and Gigante, Denise and Dierkes-Thrun, Petra. (2014). (De)constructing Salome: Toward A Dialectical Critique of Transgression. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: http://purl.stanford.edu/tp540zr1609",
